  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nTemperatures will trend warmer through Saturday as \nan upper level ridge builds overhead in the wake of a departing \ntrough today. After a cool start this morning, temperatures will \npeak near normal in the mid 60s to low 70s this afternoon. Some mid \nand high cloud will arrive this afternoon from remnant convection \nongoing over Iowa this morning. Southerly flow increases on Saturday \nas surface high pressure shifts to the east with a warm afternoon \nand temperatures solidly in the 70s. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nHurricane Isaias is forecast to make landfall Friday evening \nalong the Alabama and Florida Panhandle Coast. The tropical \nsystem merges with a weak upper trough over the western Gulf \nwith deep southerly flow providing good moisture transport north\nthrough the day on Saturday. The system is expected to weaken \nto a tropical depression within 24 hours, with the remnant low \nforecast over Central Tennessee by Sunday morning. The overall \ntrack has trended just slightly faster with rain still expected \nto reach northern Ohio after midnight Saturday night. Rain will \ntend to be moderate but could briefly be heavy at times. Breaks \nin the rain are expected to develop by Sunday afternoon as the \ndeep moisture axis starts to shift northeast and a substantial \ndry-slot wraps north ahead of the closed low. Precipitation \nstill looks to range from about a half inch to an inch and a \nquarter with locally higher amounts possible, mainly across the \nsoutheastern counties. The threat for flooding is still \nconsidered to be low due to recent dry conditions and low \nstream/river levels. \n\nAs for winds, the stronger wind field with speeds of 30-40 knots at \n850mb moves overhead between 06-18Z Sunday. Depending on the surface \npressure gradient, we could see some gusts of 20-30 mph ahead of or \nwith the rain as it arrives Saturday night. Could also see some\nhigher gusts with downsloping enhancement towards Erie Pennsylvania\nand will need to monitor trends as gusts could peak closer to \n35-45 mph towards the city. After the rain moves in, it is less \nlikely to see strong gusts with the rain stabilizing the \nboundary layer but will need to continue to monitor the track \nand strength of the low as it tracks through the Ohio Valley on \nSunday. There is a low chance of thunderstorms, mainly across \nsouthern portions of the forecast area. Additional shower \nactivity will fill back in across Northeast Ohio and Northwest \nPennsylvania Sunday afternoon/evening. The remnant low will \nquickly exit to the east Sunday night. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 3... \nAfter warming temperatures and dry conditions on Monday, the \nnext cold front is poised to settle south across the region on \nTuesday. This brings the potential for additional showers and \npossibly a few thunderstorms. Temperatures will settle back \ntowards normal behind the front on Wednesday.",
